sentences.l:
“At dawn, indeed, and still more, the son of Cronus, of surpassing strength,
you will see, if you wish, ox-eyed lady Hera,
destroying of the Argives the great army of spearmen;

for not before will he cease from war, mighty Hector,
before there rises up beside the ships the fleet-footed son of Peleus,
on that day when they by the sterns shall fight
in a most dread strait around Patroclus dead;

where Iapetus and Cronus,
sitting, neither in the glare of Hyperion the Sun
take delight, nor in winds, and deep Tartarus is round about;

And let heralds, dear to Zeus, through the town proclaim
that boys in first youth and grey-templed old men
lie down about the town on the god-built towers;

but among the foremost, I suspect,
he will lie, wounded, and many companions about him
as the sun rises, for tomorrow;
